# deploy/

Deployment artifacts for `{{.AppName}}`.

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `Dockerfile` | Three-stage build: the frontend with Node, the binary with Go embedding it, and a non-root distroless runtime image carrying nothing else. |
| `compose.yaml` | A local run of that image. |

## Building the image

```sh
docker build -f deploy/Dockerfile -t {{.AppName}} .
```

Build from the repository root, not from this directory: the Dockerfile
copies both `frontend/` and the Go tree, and the frontend build writes into
`internal/platform/webui/embedded/client/`, which the Go build then embeds.

The frontend stage runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, so
`frontend/pnpm-lock.yaml` must be committed first. Run
`pnpm --dir frontend install` once and commit the lockfile it writes; a
reproducible image needs a resolved dependency graph, and the alternative —
resolving afresh on every build — would make two builds of the same commit
differ.

## Health

The runtime image is distroless and carries no shell, so nothing inside it
can probe itself. Point your orchestrator at the three endpoints instead:
`/healthz/startup`, `/healthz/live`, and `/healthz/ready`. Shutdown fails
readiness first and then waits a drain period, so give the container a
termination grace period longer than that drain (the default is 5 seconds of
drain plus up to 30 seconds for in-flight requests).

## Configuration

Set `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` in the shell or Compose environment before running
`docker compose`. Compose refuses an absent value and uses the same value
for PostgreSQL and the application's separate `DATABASE_PASSWORD`;
it is never interpolated into a URL, baked into the image, or committed to
this file.

The image sets `APP_ENV=production`, which turns on the fail-closed startup
checks in `internal/platform/config`. It also sets `BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0` and
`ALLOW_PUBLIC_BIND=true`, because a container must bind every interface to
be reachable and the configuration refuses to do that in production without
the explicit opt-in. Every other variable is in `.env.example` at the
repository root.

A production image must **not** carry `OPERATOR_TOKEN` in this file. Supply
it through your orchestrator's secret store, either as the variable itself
or as `OPERATOR_TOKEN_FILE` naming a mounted file.

## Process modes

The same image runs every process this application has. `-mode web` binds
the HTTP port and never starts the worker; `-mode worker` starts the worker
and never binds a port; `-mode all` (the default) runs both.

```sh
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 {{.AppName}} -mode web
```
